WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.800
At 4096², FLUX.1-dev with full attention
returns stripes or a flat field.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.300
Native-size windows from step 1
draw five Moai instead of one.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.800
Up close, the stripes hold no detail.

4
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.100
Each head sees two views of the image.

5
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.300
Local: an exact native-size window
of 64×64 tokens around the query.

6
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.500
Global: one key per 4×4 block,
the whole image on a 64×64 grid.

7
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:26.700
A per-head weight π mixes the two.

8
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.300
π is fitted once, at 1024²,
where full attention still works.

9
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.600
Shrink both views by 4 (and by 2)
to mimic a 4× (2×) larger image.

10
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.800
Each head's π is the best fit
to full attention, in closed form.

11
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.800
One π for each of 1368 heads,
noise level and ratio.

12
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.600
Heads differ: most lean local,
a few lean global.

13
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.900
Mixing windows in from step 1
draws five Moai.

14
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.900
SCA runs 8 of 28 steps globally,
then shrinks its windows over 4 steps.

15
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.600
The opening keeps one large statue;
a small second Moai remains far off.

16
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.200
Steps 1 to 8 skip the local branch:
every head is global.

17
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.000
Then the windows shrink, 128 to 80 tokens;
each head's π is read at that ratio.

18
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.800
The last 16 steps use native windows. Every
window also sees 1,024 bridge tokens.

19
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.000
The final 4096² image,
with a 1:1 crop of its detail.

